        624.0654296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "i=1",
      "bbox": [
        285.760986328125,
        635.439697265625,
        299.45941162109375,
        642.5171508789062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "f(x, µi, σi) −γ",
      "bbox": [
        302.30999755859375,
        619.7021484375,
        371.794921875,
        631.066162109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(11)",
      "bbox": [
        503.82501220703125,
        621.224609375,
        521.9995727539062,
        630.9773559570312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Therefore, θ = (µ1, . . . , µm; σ1, . . . , σm; γ).",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        654.6390380859375,
        285.1302490234375,
        665.3931884765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Figure 1 demonstrates that RBFs are particularly well-suited for constructing reachable sets due",
      "bbox": [
        106.93598937988281,
        668.3666381835938,
        522.0051879882812,
        678.119384765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to the tail interactions that allow multiple RBFs to connect into shapes more complicated than unions",
      "bbox": [
        89.99998474121094,
        681.9166259765625,
        522.000244140625,
        691.6693725585938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of ellipsoids. This approach allows the number of RBFs, m, to be arbitrarily set. If m is larger than",
      "bbox": [
        89.99998474121094,
        695.2910766601562,
        521.9956665039062,
        705.2183837890625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
